  <p class="iop-essay-label">
    WEEK 05 / ESSAY ARCHIVE
  </p>

  <h3>
    Passenger Seat: Driven Crazy Because I Can’t Drive Anywhere Else
  </h3>

  <p>
    “Slow down!” I squeezed my eyes shut as we took another turn at
    10 mph too fast. Luckily, it wasn’t a hairpin; otherwise, I’d be
    thrown against the window. I was already holding on to both handles.
    “We’re going to miss the football game—halftime is over already!”
    my roommate said as he sped. “Close your eyes now.” I sighed.
    “Does this mean you’re passing someone on a double yellow again?”
    The engine revved as he lead-footed the gas. <em>Typical</em>,
    I thought. <em>He’s driving like he’s trying to kill me again.</em>
    But I was used to this walk-the-line-between-life-and-death driving
    by now since Sarel had been driving me around for about nine months
    by this point. He always took the turns in the canyons too fast
    and passed people in risky areas. He always said, “I know where
    to pass.” So did I, since I had lived up in Jamestown for four
    years, but I also knew not to pass in those places because people
    drive like idiots up in the mountains. I was sitting in the
    passenger seat and had been for just under two years, and I
    couldn’t complain because it was all my fault. I was lucky to
    even have a ride and be on an outing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rewind to July 3, 2024. My eyes opened to ugly white cinderblock
    walls, my back on a cheap mat masquerading as a bed with sheets
    laundered by Cintas, and a few other empty beds in the same room
    without a door. The bathroom looked weirdly clean, sterile, and
    like it was transplanted from a psych ward. I walked out to find
    some weird control booth. “You’re in detox. You got a DUI last
    night, and the police brought you here instead of jail.”
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>Oh.</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    I stayed in detox for hours until they cleared me to leave.
    To fill the time, I read crusty books on sobriety, begged for
    nicotine gum, watched the minute hand on a clock, and wished
    for earplugs to block the cacophonous blasting noise of the
    hand dryers. Eventually, I was picked up.
  </p>

  <p>
    Due to the mania side of a mixed episode, impulsivity, and the
    idiotic decision to drink and drive, I blacked out and decided
    to try to commit suicide. I hit another car at low speed—no
    injuries, thankfully—and was a complete mess, apparently,
    according to the police report. I totaled my Subaru Legacy.
    I had my day in court, and when I was arrested in court and
    sent straight to jail, it was a surprise to everyone. I was
    in jail for five months and got out with no car, no license,
    and no way to get anywhere but to beg for a spot in the passenger
    seat. Otherwise, I was housebound, and my way to get anything
    done was a Zoom call.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything was done on someone else’s schedule, availability,
    and kindness to take me somewhere. It got old very fast.
    I hadn’t seen the sunset from the flatlands in months. Night
    drives were a thing of the past. All of a sudden, I had to beg
    for rides to get cigarettes and groceries. If I had an
    appointment that I couldn’t get a ride to, I had to cancel
    and reschedule. I couldn’t just buy another car like I was
    replacing a lightbulb. My dependence was definitely not an
    ascendance. I was coping with the grief of losing my
    independence along with everything else in my life.
  </p>

  <p>
    Grief is often compared to a roller coaster because of its
    unpredictability, emotional twists and turns, and lack of a
    straightforward path. Feelings of progress can be abruptly
    replaced by anger, sadness, or frustration. Disorientation,
    uncertainty, and a sense of being lost are often experienced
    throughout the grieving process. Like a roller coaster, grief
    can become more manageable when attempts to control every twist
    and turn are abandoned and the experience is accepted.
    Acceptance should not be mistaken for enjoyment or approval
    of the circumstances that caused the grief. Instead, it
    involves acknowledging that not every emotional high or low
    can be prevented, but that each can be experienced and
    navigated without resistance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Losing my independence—the things I had done, the things I had
    lost, knowing I had hurt people—caused me a lot of grief and
    sadness. Even though nobody was hurt in the accident, even
    though my parents weren’t in the car with me, even though
    I didn’t spend the night in jail, my car was still totaled,
    people were still affected by my actions, and I was stuck
    at home. I was no longer able to do things I used to be able
    to, and possibilities for my future were gone: having a job,
    dating, making new friends, going on hikes and camping,
    snowshoeing… gone, in just a few seconds. In just a day or
    over the span of hours, my moods swung like Sammy Sosa at
    a home run derby. I felt anger at myself, sadness for my
    circumstances, resignation. I felt acceptance at some points,
    too. My moods changed so often they would’ve broken a mood ring.
  </p>

  <p>
    At this point, when I was sitting at home with my thumb up
    my ass waiting for someone to be ready to drive me somewhere
    or for a ride to arrive to take me somewhere, I knew I
    couldn’t undo my DUI, the accident, or being locked up after
    I came home. I knew I could regret everything and wish things
    had happened differently, playing the tape over and over
    again in my head as if that would change anything. I stopped
    doing that eventually, as it wore me out and only made my
    brain fry. Another thing that stopped was wishing people
    would do things on my timeline—I prepared for things by
    asking to do things on other people’s schedules and preparing
    in advance. Adapting was easier than wishful thinking.
  </p>

  <p>
    One good coping strategy is Riding Waves from DBT. It emphasizes
    that emotions and impulses are temporary, like waves in the
    ocean. They rise, reach their peak, and eventually fall.
    For example, when feeling hopeless and helpless about dependence,
    along with the urge to make an impulsive decision or do something
    stupid, it’s important to remember that experiencing these
    emotions doesn’t mean that acting on them is a required
    assignment and is graded for homework. Practice some
    techniques such as mindfulness, breathing, relaxation,
    or other distress-tolerance techniques. The circumstances
    may remain the same, but the response to them can be managed.
    Urge surfing means recognizing the frustration without giving
    it the steering wheel, acknowledging that the emotion will
    pass even if the situation hasn’t been resolved.
  </p>

  <p>
    Another DBT technique for managing overwhelming emotions is
    SERENITY. The letters stand for:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Slow Your Thoughts:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Slow thoughts to think clearly, listen more intently,
          and respond rather than react.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Effective Behavior:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Figure out what the next best step is to act effectively
          and which spiritual principles to apply to this situation
          to move to Wise Mind.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Relax and Breathe:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Being able to relax the body to quiet the mind;
          slowing down thoughts to relax the body.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Stay Grounded in Your Environment:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Stay focused and aware of the present.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Notice How Others Respond to You:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Notice how others respond when reacting rather than
          responding.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Imagine Yourself Successfully Responding to the Situation:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Remember that having the skills to successfully respond
          to others, even in challenging situations, is possible.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Tolerate Your Traits:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Focus on skills and strengths instead of traits
          that are disliked.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>You Can Do This:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Positive self-talk helps with learning to respond
          versus react.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Losing independence can cause emotions to spiral: anger,
    regret, anxiety. Slowing down thoughts and practicing mindful
    breathing can help prevent an emotional reaction from turning
    into an impulsive decision or thoughts from jumping around,
    going haywire. It prevents obsession over circumstances and
    a past that cannot be changed no matter how many thoughts,
    tears, or tantrums happen, such as a DUI, a suicide attempt,
    a mismanaged manic impulsive decision, or loss of driving
    privileges. The key is to stay grounded in the present; this
    prevents past mistakes and fears about the future from
    consuming the mind and moment. Another essential consideration
    is noticing how others respond. This is important when relying
    on friends, family, and neighbors for transportation because
    they are helping, and it’s important to be patient and kind
    to the help.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is a need to acknowledge shortcomings without letting
    them define an entire identity and to use positive self-talk
    to encourage progress rather than perfection. SERENITY emphasizes
    all of that while, at the same time, consequences of past decisions
    cannot be undone, and responsibility can still be taken for
    present behavior. Accountability does not require endless
    self-punishment and self-castigation. Accepting the situation
    does not mean giving up on changing it—a classic example
    of a dialectic.
  </p>

  <p>
    It’s November 2026. After two and a half years of getting
    in the right side of a car, staring at glove boxes, moving
    fast-food wrappers off the floor, and being envious of people
    turning keys in ignitions, I’m ready to go grocery shopping
    at 6:30 am, peruse every aisle, go shopping at Goodwill and
    take my time, waste time at Five Below, and make a weekly
    run to Michael’s. I know it’ll end eventually, and I’ve
    come to accept my circumstances of being at the mercy of
    others’ driving: stopping at yellow lights, driving 5 mph
    under the speed limit, driving too fast around hairpins
    in the mountain roads, enduring endless complaining about
    bicycles in the road (well, I can tolerate this one).
    I do find myself unwillingly on the roller coaster sometimes,
    though, going through the loops, twists, and turns—going
    upside down, through the tunnels, being launched from the
    starting platform at 60 mph. I’d just like to take the
    harness off and use it when Sarel drives me around for
    now—until I get my own wheels and can go the speed limit,
    listen to my own music, and go thrifting.
  </p>

</article>
